            <ArticleTitle>Glenoidectomy for treatment of a comminuted scapular fracture in a cat.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>2055116915613816</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1177/2055116915613816</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="CASE SUMMARY" NlmCategory="METHODS">This case report describes the surgical technique used and clinical outcome of a 15-year-old neutered female cat that had a comminuted fracture of the right glenoid and scapular neck secondary to a gunshot injury that was treated with glenoidectomy.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RELEVANCE AND NOVEL INFORMATION" NlmCategory="UNASSIGNED">Good clinical outcomes are possible with removal of the glenoid for treatment of comminuted fractures of the scapulohumeral joint. Glenoidectomy is a viable alternative to amputation in cats with normal neurovascular supply to the affected limb. Persistent functional, pain-free lameness, muscle hypertrophy and changes in shoulder range of motion are to be expected.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
